Maya's journey surfaces a pattern common to independent learners: curiosity is cheap, but follow-through is hard. The app's real job isn't just to store what she finds — it's to close the gap between discovery and practice.

This pointed toward two concrete opportunities: active pronunciation practice with real feedback, and a structured way to build and revisit personal word collections. Less a content library, more a personal training ground.

The "holding space for curiosity" idea — saving articles, bookmarking resources — remains interesting as a peripheral feature. But it's not the core.

What Maya actually needs is a system that meets her where her motivation already is, and makes the step from "I heard this word" to "I can say this word" as small as possible.
